<section class="space-y-6 leading-relaxed">
  <p class="border-primary border-l-4 py-2 pl-4 text-xl italic opacity-60">
    "Team development doesn't start with a grand plan. It starts with the first
    moment people meet."
  </p>

  <p>
    Whoever shapes a team shapes relationships — consciously or unconsciously.
    The more powerful approach is to actively steer this process. Here are six
    things you can do, ideally in this order.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-10 mb-4 text-2xl font-semibold">
    1. Conscious Start: Kick-off with Real Encounters
  </h2>

  <p>
    At the beginning, one thing matters most:
    <strong>creating connection</strong>. A good kick-off is more than a project
    launch. It's a relationship moment. Icebreakers or story-sharing rounds
    invite people to show more than just their roles — they reveal who they are.
  </p>

  <p>
    Who am I? What motivates me? How do I prefer to work? These insights lay the
    foundation for trust and open conversations about what the team wants to
    achieve together. A team charter and a clear goal belong at the start — not
    as formalities, but as expressions of direction and intention.
  </p>

  <div class="card-bordered my-8">
    <h3 class="mb-2 font-semibold">In the game</h3>
    <p class="text-sm opacity-80">
      Level 1 tasks — walking together, mind maps, finding commonalities, and
      creating the first team charter — embody this principle. They're designed
      to create connection before diving into anything harder.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 class="mt-10 mb-4 text-2xl font-semibold">
    2. Cultivate Relationships and Understanding in Daily Work
  </h2>

  <p>
    Team development happens "on the job" — in the small things, again and
    again. In how teams meet each other in meetings. In conversations between
    door and wall. In moments when someone says: "I see you." Invest in shared
    time beyond work and in clarifying shared understanding.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-10 mb-4 text-2xl font-semibold">
    3. Workshops as Conscious Development Spaces
  </h2>

  <p>
    Sometimes more space is needed for reflection, learning, and new
    perspectives. Good workshops offer not just methods but
    <strong>protected spaces</strong> where teams can question and shape their
    collaboration. Ideally, over the course of its development, a team makes a
    shared learning experience in each of the structural intervention areas:
    perception, communication, decisions, roles, and conflict.
  </p>

  <div class="card-bordered my-8">
    <h3 class="mb-2 font-semibold">In the game</h3>
    <p class="text-sm opacity-80">
      Level 8 includes planning a joint training. The game doesn't prescribe
      what — it asks the team to choose what they need most. This respects that
      every team's development path is unique.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 class="mt-10 mb-4 text-2xl font-semibold">
    4. Regularly Renew Team Goal and Charter
  </h2>

  <p>
    What was clarified at the start is not set in stone. Teams evolve: goals
    shift, new members join, others leave. Regularly reflecting on the charter
    and team goal keeps integration intact. The game reflects this by revisiting
    the team charter at every second level, each time at a deeper layer.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-10 mb-4 text-2xl font-semibold">
    5. Establish Retrospectives with Relationship Time
  </h2>

  <p>
    Retrospectives are more than reviews. They are training for togetherness.
    When teams run them regularly and expand them — with moments of gratitude or
    small personal insights — trust grows through repetition. Those who
    regularly pause recognize not only patterns but also people, developing the
    capacity to shape tensions together.
  </p>

  <div class="card-bordered my-8">
    <h3 class="mb-2 font-semibold">In the game</h3>
    <p class="text-sm opacity-80">
      Every even-numbered level (2, 4, 6, 8, 10) includes a retrospective task.
      Each one focuses on a different question: collaboration, trust, goal
      progress, conflict, and difficult situations. This keeps reflection as a
      rhythm, not an event.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 class="mt-10 mb-4 text-2xl font-semibold">
    6. Shape Team Identity Together
  </h2>

  <p>
    Teams that take time to talk about their values, language, and self-image
    gain more than a nice vision statement: they develop a
    <strong>shared narrative</strong> that carries them. Whether through
    symbols, rituals, or inside jokes — identity forms where people recognize
    themselves in each other.
  </p>

  <div class="card-bordered my-8">
    <h3 class="mb-2 font-semibold">In the game</h3>
    <p class="text-sm opacity-80">
      Level 7 is dedicated entirely to identity: a team photo, a 5-sentence
      self-portrait, an information radiator, and a charter on what you want to
      stand for. This is the midpoint of the year — a natural time to look
      inward as a team.
    </p>
  </div>

  <section class="border-base-300 mt-16 border-t pt-8">
    <h2 class="mb-4 text-xl font-semibold">Putting It All Together</h2>
    <p class="opacity-80">
      Team development is not a linear project with a fixed end date. It's an
      ongoing practice of paying attention to how people work together. The six
      steps above form a cycle, not a checklist. Run through them, revisit them,
      adapt them to your team's rhythm.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 opacity-80">
      The DIY Team Building game gives you a structure for a year of this
      practice. The lessons give you the understanding behind it. But the real
      work — showing up, being curious, staying with the complexity — that's
      yours to do.
    </p>
  </section>
</section>
